From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] locks: add open file description locks (F_OFD_GETLK,
 F_OFD_SETLK, F_OFD_SETLKW)

Open file description (OFD) locks are byte range locks that conflict with
classic POSIX record locks but are owned by the open file description
(the struct file) instead of the process: they are not dropped when the
process closes some other fd of the same file, they are shared by dup()ed
fds and by every process that holds a reference to the same open file
(fork(), SCM_RIGHTS, binder), and they go away when the last such
reference is put. Linux has had them since 3.15; this kernel answers
EINVAL.

Why: Android's MediaProvider FUSE daemon (packages/providers/
MediaProvider/jni/FuseDaemon.cpp, set_file_lock()/is_file_locked() called
from ShouldOpenWithFuse() and pf_open) takes an F_OFD_SETLK read lock on
the lower filesystem fd before handing it to an app, and later asks with
F_OFD_GETLK whether such an fd is still open anywhere. On this kernel
both calls fail ("W FuseDaemon: Failed to set lock: read: Invalid
argument", "Failed to check lock: Invalid argument"), so MediaProvider
never returns lower filesystem fds and opens every FUSE handle with
direct_io. Two consequences seen on build 9: the camera app's video
recording fails with EFAULT (fuse direct_io does get_user_pages() on the
Codec2 ION/dmabuf buffer the muxer writes from, a VM_IO|VM_PFNMAP
mapping) and stops after a fraction of a second, and all media file I/O
goes through FUSE without the page cache.

Derived from upstream, the 3.15 series by Jeff Layton
