What the result does (all read in the final code):
- F_OFD_SETLK/F_OFD_SETLKW/F_OFD_GETLK are 37/38/36
  (include/uapi/asm-generic/fcntl.h). On this 32-bit kernel they are
  accepted by sys_fcntl64() and take a struct flock64, as upstream; the
  legacy sys_fcntl() keeps answering EINVAL for them on 32-bit (upstream
  did not add the new interface to the legacy entry point, and bionic
  always calls fcntl64 on LP32).
- The owner of an OFD lock is the struct file: fcntl_setlk()/setlk64()
  and fcntl_getlk()/getlk64() set FL_OFDLCK and fl_owner = filp, then
  continue as F_SETLK/F_SETLKW/F_GETLK(64). posix_same_owner() compares
  fl_owner, so an OFD lock and a classic lock of the same process have
  different owners and conflict, an OFD lock taken through any fd of the
  same description (dup, fork, passed fd) merges with it, and F_OFD_GETLK
  does not report the caller's own description's locks.
- l_pid must be 0 on input (EINVAL otherwise); a conflicting OFD lock is
  reported with l_pid = -1 by both F_GETLK and F_OFD_GETLK.
- Release: locks_remove_file() (the renamed locks_remove_flock(), called
  from __fput() on the last reference to the struct file) first calls
  locks_remove_posix(filp, (fl_owner_t)filp). filp_close() still calls
  locks_remove_posix(filp, files) on every close, which only matches
  classic locks, so closing some other fd of the file leaves OFD locks
  alone.
- F_OFD_SETLKW sleeps in do_lock_file_wait() with
  wait_event_interruptible() like F_SETLKW and returns EINTR on a
  signal.
- Deadlock detection is skipped for OFD requests
  (posix_locks_deadlock() returns 0) and waiters blocked on an OFD lock
  are not put on blocked_list, as upstream.
- /proc/locks prints "OFDLCK" in the type column instead of "POSIX ",
  same width and field count; classic lines are unchanged. The pid
  column shows the pid that set the lock (3.15 behaviour; kernels since
  4.14 print -1 there).
- Mandatory locking (unused on this system: needs -o mand) also treats
  OFD locks held through the same file as the caller's own.
- CIFS, FUSE and other ->lock() filesystems receive the request through
  vfs_lock_file() with the converted cmd, as upstream. For FUSE the
  MediaProvider daemon does not implement getlk/setlk, so the kernel
  keeps the locks locally (fc->no_lock) and everything above applies to
  files on /sdcard too.

Adapted for 3.10:
- fs/locks.c of 3.10 still uses the global file_lock_lock, the single
  blocked_list and fl_link for the blocked list: the "do not add waiters
  of OFD locks to the global blocked list" change is applied to
  locks_insert_block() (upstream: __locks_insert_block() and the
  blocked_hash of 3.12+).
- file_inode() is defined later in include/linux/fs.h than
  locks_verify_locked(); it is moved up before the file locking
  declarations (upstream moved it next to ihold()/iput(), which in 3.10
  come after the locking block). The callers of locks_verify_locked()
  are the same three as upstream (fs/namei.c, mm/mmap.c, mm/nommu.c).
- The f_mode vs lock type check stays where 3.10 has it, in
  fcntl_setlk()/setlk64() after the cmd switch (upstream moved it around
  in bce7560d4946/cf01f4eef9fe with the same effect).
- No fs/compat.c hunk: this kernel has no CONFIG_COMPAT.
- arch/arm/kernel/sys_oabi-compat.c already referenced F_OFD_GETLK in
  do_locks() (from a stable backport) without it being defined anywhere;
  CONFIG_OABI_COMPAT is off here so it never compiled. The OFD cases are
  added to sys_oabi_fcntl64() as upstream; still not compiled.
- Everything else applies as upstream; fl_owner_t is still
  "struct files_struct *" in 3.10, the (fl_owner_t)filp casts are the
  3.15 ones.

Compile-tested (ARM, GCC 4.9, lineage_gts210ltexx_defconfig): zImage
builds, no warnings in the touched files. A static ARM test program for
the semantics above is in staging/ofd-locks/tests (its logic verified
on a 7.3 host kernel). Not run on the device.
